p.src:
The next movement is one of the lecture’s most important. Sheru and John redefine the coercion at issue. Poverty, illness, and blocked opportunity are themselves coercive conditions. If people live under those burdens, they are not truly free. What looked like a straightforward libertarian moral victory now becomes an argument about competing accounts of freedom: $\text{lack of health care, poverty, lack of education}
\Longrightarrow
\text{not truly free}.$

