p.src:
Skyler then supplies the most explicit practical argument. If we provide preventive care now, we reduce later costs and improve public health for everyone. That reasoning can be written as a short derivation: $\begin{aligned}
\text{preventive care now}
&\Longrightarrow
\text{fewer untreated illnesses later}, \notag\\
&\Longrightarrow
\text{lower treatment burden} + \text{lower contagion risk}, \notag\\
&\Longrightarrow
\text{higher public health}.\end{aligned}$ So we obtain the lecture’s practical schema: $\text{public health coverage}
\Longrightarrow
\text{lower long-run costs} + \text{higher public health}.$

