p.src:
Now Sandel pauses and performs one of the lecture’s most important acts of reorganization. The long audience exchange, which could easily sprawl, is reduced to three distinct arguments: $\begin{aligned}
\text{three arguments for taxpayer-supported health care}
&=
\{\text{practical},\ \text{freedom},\ \text{common good}\}, \notag\\
\text{practical}
&:
\text{coverage}
\Longrightarrow
\text{lower costs} + \text{higher public health}, \notag\\
\text{freedom}
&:
\text{illness without care}
\Longrightarrow
\text{not truly free}, \notag\\
\text{common good}
&:
\text{citizenship}
\Longrightarrow
\text{mutual responsibilities}. \notag\end{aligned}$

