p.src:
One side says that redistribution is coercive because it forcibly takes from some their hard-won earnings and gives them to others. The other side says that people burdened by illness, poverty, or lack of education are not truly free. Much of the lecture can therefore be read as a dispute internal to freedom itself: $\text{American welfare-state argument}
\Longrightarrow
\text{often framed in the language of freedom}.$

